  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $2$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fourths*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/51c7388ea0207faad61a17f243a78db4a1ce7cd6e24ee869851bb4e548871db3.png)\n\n \n\n- The fourths are grouped, and each group contains $\\frac{2}{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/1bd9e191e335d532b593b69c87f396cf881c1feb70e19749975e5a07828d5d0c.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $4$ groups in total.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/4452acb030b7089b69860cd77c2285b27b373db7577fd4856f0abec7d5f5c951.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $2$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{2}{4},$ we get $4$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,2\\,}÷\\frac{2}{4}=\\boxed{\\,4\\,}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $2$ and $4.$",
